so long as sleeps the might of the North Wind and of other
very violent winds, which the shadowy clouds
with clear breaths scatter, blowing;

those two, then, seizing cattle and stout flocks,
the steadings of men ravaged, until they themselves too
in the hands of men were killed with sharp bronze;

And the two, fallen, pitied Ares-loved Menelaus,
and went through the front-fighters, armed with the fire-faced bronze,
shaking his spear;

and he stood, coming very close, and cast with a shining spear,
and hit Amphius, Selagus’ son, who in Paesus
dwelt, of many possessions, of many cornfields;

Him then at the belt hit Telamonian Ajax,
and in the lower belly was fixed the long-shadowed spear,
and he fell with a thud;
